doc.para:
if Statement
　The if statement is a syntax structure that determines whether or not to execute a portion of a script based on a conditional judgment, such as "if XX, do YY".
　The syntax is as follows:

if(expression)
	statement or block
else
	statement or block

　The first "statement or block" is executed when the result of evaluating expression is true, and the second "statement or block" is executed when the evaluation result is false. You do not need to write the else clause and what follows if it is not necessary.

	if(a==b)
		inform("a and b are the same");

	if(a<b)
	{
		var t;
		t=a; a=b; b=t; // Swap a and b 
	}

	if(a==b)
		inform("a and b are the same");
	else
		inform("a and b are different");

Correspondence between if and else
　else follows the rule "corresponds to the preceding if that does not yet have a corresponding else".

For example, if you write:

	if(expr) // ★
		if(expr) // ●
			statement;
		else // ●
			statement;
	else // ★
		statement;

　the ★ else corresponds to the ★ if, and the ● else corresponds to the ● if.
　In free-form languages like TJS2, even if you make an indentation error and write:

	if(expr) // ★
		if(expr) // ●
			statement;
	else // ●
		statement;

　you must be careful because the correspondence remains as described above.
　If you want to clearly delimit the correspondence,

	if(expr) { // ★
		if(expr) // ●
			statement;
	}
	else // ★
		statement;

　please adopt the method of enclosing it in a block like this.
